# ThreadSanitizer suppressions. The goal is an empty file; every entry is a
# claim that a report is not ours, and needs to say why.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# crossbeam-epoch / crossbeam-deque, reached only through rayon's work-stealing
# scheduler.
#
# What the reports look like: the racing accesses are inside
# `crossbeam_epoch::internal::Global::{collect,try_advance}`,
# `crossbeam_epoch::deferred::Deferred::call` (epoch-based reclamation freeing a
# `Local` or a `SealedBag`) and `crossbeam_deque::Stealer::steal`. Vx symbols
# appear in the stacks only as the frame that *enters* rayon --
# `vxc::resolver::build_symbol_map` calling `par_iter`, a test building a pool --
# never as a participant in the racing access itself. No Vx datum is involved.
#
# Why they are not real: crossbeam-epoch synchronises with
# `atomic::fence(SeqCst)` paired with relaxed loads, and TSan does not fully
# model `atomic_thread_fence` -- it misses the happens-before edge the fence
# establishes and reports the pair as a race. This is a known and long-standing
# limitation of the detector, not of the library.
#
# Why suppressing is safe *here*: Vx does not use crossbeam directly. It is
# reachable only via rayon, so an entry scoped to crossbeam cannot hide a race
# in compiler code.
#
# Note the shape of the evidence: these reports appear when running the **test
# suite**, which builds and tears down a rayon pool per test and so hammers
# epoch reclamation. The compiler sweep itself -- 300 modules at 1/8/32/48
# threads, both interning modes -- reports nothing at all, suppressions or not.
race:crossbeam_epoch
race:crossbeam_deque
